WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.755
Last video for week five.

2
00:00:01.805 --> 00:00:04.282
Everything so far was about the agent.

3
00:00:04.332 --> 00:00:15.475
This one is about the organisation around it: who owns the agent, who is accountable when it causes harm, and the two documents that every AI professional is expected to know.

4
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:18.624
Strip governance down and it is three questions.

5
00:00:18.674 --> 00:00:22.911
Who owns this agent, by name, and can switch it off in an hour?

6
00:00:22.961 --> 00:00:28.729
What is it allowed to do, and where is that written down in a form the owner has signed?

7
00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:33.015
And when it causes harm, who finds out, how fast, and what happens next?

8
00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:38.527
If your team cannot answer all three for your project, the decision memo cannot honestly recommend deploying it.

9
00:00:39.277 --> 00:00:48.357
The first document is the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, version 1.0, published in January 2023 by the US standards agency.

10
00:00:48.407 --> 00:00:52.704
It is voluntary, and it is organised around four functions.

11
00:00:52.754 --> 00:00:54.878
Govern: policies, roles and accountability.

12
00:00:54.928 --> 00:00:59.660
Map: understand the context and the risks of a specific system.

13
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.399
Measure: test and monitor.

14
00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:04.007
Manage: act on what you measured.

15
00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:07.051
Your project maps onto it almost exactly.

16
00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:09.224
The security review is map.

17
00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:11.398
The evaluation report is measure.

18
00:01:11.448 --> 00:01:14.876
The operations sheet and the gates are manage.

19
00:01:14.926 --> 00:01:18.789
The owner's name on the decision memo is govern.

20
00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:22.220
The second document is binding law.

21
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:35.874
The EU AI Act, Regulation 2024/1689, applies to AI systems used in the European Union regardless of where they were built, and its obligations scale with risk.

22
00:01:35.924 --> 00:01:38.605
A handful of uses are banned.

23
00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:48.618
High-risk uses, including hiring, credit decisions, education and access to essential services, carry requirements for documentation, human oversight, logging and accuracy testing.

24
00:01:48.668 --> 00:01:58.631
Most agents you will build are lower risk, but notice that the high-risk requirements are the same artefacts this course asks for.

25
00:01:58.681 --> 00:02:00.452
Documentation, oversight, logs, testing.

26
00:02:00.502 --> 00:02:03.637
A well-run agent service already has them.

27
00:02:04.388 --> 00:02:07.575
One more point, and people forget it all the time.

28
00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:09.193
The agent cannot be accountable.

29
00:02:09.243 --> 00:02:12.106
It has no budget, no job and no signature.

30
00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:19.552
Someone decided to deploy it, with certain tools, at a certain position on the dial, and that decision should be on the record.

31
00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:28.292
So your decision memo names people: an owner who can switch the agent off, an approver for each gate, and a reviewer who reads the weekly sample.

32
00:02:28.342 --> 00:02:30.558
Write a person's name in each role.

33
00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:32.824
A team name is not an answer.

34
00:02:32.874 --> 00:02:38.003
When the Replit agent deleted that database, customers did not ask what the model was thinking.

35
00:02:38.053 --> 00:02:40.593
They asked who had given it that tool.

36
00:02:41.343 --> 00:02:53.494
So for this week's deliverable: map your controls to the OWASP names and the four NIST functions, state the risk tier you believe applies and why, and name the owner, the approvers and the reviewer.

37
00:02:53.544 --> 00:02:56.282
In class this week is the midpoint demo.

38
00:02:56.332 --> 00:02:59.420
Show one gate working, live, on your own agent.

39
00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:06.391
Next week we move from safety to running the thing: cost, observability, and the decision to build, buy or wait.
